    "title": "New boarding bridge debuts at Joplin Regional Airport - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Aidan Garvey ,\n5 hours ago\nJOPLIN, Mo. — A new piece of airport equipment was put to use this morning, one that will simplify the travel process for both passengers and staff alike.\nJoplin Regional Airport is home to a new boarding bridge. The bridge was put to use for the first time this morning and will aid travelers in their journeys to both hub airports in Denver and Chicago.\nA boarding bridge, while existing as a norm in larger airports, is not as common in smaller hubs like Joplin, but officials say the new addition will help to set Joplin apart going forward.\n\"I'm not sure if there's another sized airport that's similar that I can think of that has a boarding bridge like this, but for the most part, small community airports like Joplin, especially in EAS markets, a lot of them do not have boarding bridges, and that's what sets us apart from other airports that are our size,\" said Airport Manager Alison Kramer.\nOn a related note, the city and SkyWest Airlines have submitted separate proposals to the U.S. Department of Transportation regarding a switch to one of their travel hubs. While the Denver hub would remain untouched, the proposal would move the Eastern Hub of O'Hare Airport in Chicago to George Bush Intercontinental in Houston.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed.\nFor the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KSNF/KODE | FourStatesHomepage.com.",
    "summary": "— A new piece of airport equipment was put to use this morning, one that will simplify the travel process for both passengers and staff alike. The bridge was put to use for the first time this morning and will aid travelers in their journeys to both hub airports in Denver and Chicago. A boarding bridge, while existing as a norm in larger airports, is not as common in smaller hubs like Joplin, but officials say the new addition will help to set Joplin apart going forward.",
